HLS quality selection and encoding cost
=======================================

Only source quality copies the original video stream. Low, medium and high
encode H.264 output and apply the existing configured quality/rate settings,
regardless of whether the input is H.264 or HEVC. Low targets 640x360 and
500 kbit/s maximum video rate; medium targets 1280x720 and 1500 kbit/s; high
retains the input resolution with a 3000 kbit/s maximum video rate. These are
encoder settings, not guaranteed total network bandwidth (audio, muxing and
transport add overhead). Source preserves video codec and resolution and
requires a client that supports that codec. Audio handling is unchanged.

Previously all H.264 inputs were copied, even when low/medium/high was requested.
Correctly applying these presets costs CPU. Multi-camera low-quality views now
perform the requested encoding; size installations for simultaneous transcodes.
This correction does not add hardware acceleration, admission limits, per-viewer
leases or an aggregate CPU/memory quota. Those remain separate release work.

The native regression fixture generates owned H.264 and HEVC clips, replaces
only RTSP input with those files, and renders live/recorded playlists with the
actual service and encoder arguments. Decoded frame dimensions verify low and
medium resizing. Separately rendered source-mode segment bytes establish that
high also encodes while source retains its video packets. The fixture limits
threads and checks child/task cleanup. A local tool pass does not qualify the
shipped image: packaged FFmpeg checks must run without skips. This is not a
camera, RTSP-network, production-concurrency or hardware-decoder acceptance test.
